{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1449412-character-concepts-and-storytellers","id":1449412,"title":"Character concepts and Storytellers","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-05-23T19:17:06-07:00","id":1449412,"title":"Character concepts and Storytellers","content":"So I invited some players to a game. I lay out the genre, sub-genre and general tone I'm going for.<br /> <br /> ...and one player presents a concept that is just. Wildly outside of any of that. They give me effective pages of character history that involves multiple multiples of child abuse, mental illness and near-death experiences.<br /> What's more, they give me the beginnings of a whole mythology; this Greater Destiny the character seems preordained for.<br /> ...and then they give me a couple lines at the end explaining how this concept can be shoe-horned into the game I'm planning.<br /> <br /> It just feels so out of place, so incompatible. But I don't want to say &quot;no&quot; outright. I figure, if you want a character with a certain feel, we can work together to hit that tone within the frame of the greater narrative.<br /> So I ask since questions, offer some tangential ideas and compromises. And the result is basically &quot;no. My character is like this, exactly.&quot;<br /> <br /> After some back-and-forth, the player says they'll shelve the character if it just won't fit the story. But--and I'm just guessing, here--the player would feel snubbed if I took them up on this offer.<br /> <br /> I'm curious what people here do in situations like this. It seems to be an issue specifically with modern game settings; you have some players who only know how to make wizards and knights and other over-the-top characters with suitably over-the-top backgrounds. A character concept like &quot;jaded ex-cop&quot; or &quot;paranoid cryptozoologist&quot; seems boring to them. But I feel like modern horror games come to life when more or less regular folk are forced to face extraordinary circumstances.<br /> I also feel like there's a bit of a &quot;I'm a writer&quot; sort of complex going on as well. But I really don't feel like tangling with that aspect, if it can be at all avoided.<br /> <br /> What do you think?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26844-quixote","name":"Quixote","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-23T21:41:40-07:00","id":1449421,"title":"","content":"Group roleplaying is a cooperative hobby. The Storyteller has responsibilities to work together with the players and the players have responsibilities to work together with the Storyteller and with each other.<br /> <br /> There are a lot of problems that can be pointed to here. &quot;Multiple instances of child abuse and mental illness&quot; not only contributes to the concept's over-the-top nature, it's also very dark material that not every group will be comfortable with exploring. The &quot;greater destiny&quot; roadmap speaks of laying things out for the Storyteller's plans. This is not intrinsically bad, it's <i>great</i> to have a player share where they see their character's story going and what they're interested in exploring, but the story is a <i>conversation</i> between the Storyteller and the player, and it sounds like the player has not put forth a springboard from which you can bounce ideas off each other and come to something that interests both of you (and the other players, they're along for the ride too after all), but the final word which can be taken or left, without room for input or flexibility.<br /> <br /> Inflexibility is a problem. Improvisation and the unexpected are a huge part of roleplaying.<br /> <br /> I usually suggest, when starting a game, to begin not with character creation but a conversation about what you (that is to say, all of you as a group) want the game to be about and like, what interests you, and finally after that, the rough outline of what the characters have in common that binds them together as a party. <i>Then</i> you find out who the characters actually are, after you've established a social and genre context for them. Starting first with character creation and then figuring out how to fit the sheets together into a game can yield atomized characters who don't fit into each other's stories well, and this sounds like an extreme worst case scenario.<br /> <br /> Personally, I'd also just turn down any concept that comes in the form of a several-page writeup unless it was super gripping and exactly what I wanted to do. I can't promise I'm going to read all that, absorb it, and revisit all of its elements in play, especially not when I need to keep room to spotlight everybody else's characters' stuff too. That's writing a check I can't clear and it's not fair to me, to the other players, or to the player who wrote the concept.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-24T06:26:18-07:00","id":1449452,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Stupid Loserman</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1449412-character-concepts-and-storytellers?p=1449421#post1449421\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I usually suggest, when starting a game, to begin not with character creation but a conversation about what you (that is to say, all of you as a group) want the game to be about and like, what interests you, and finally after that, the rough outline of what the characters have in common that binds them together as a party. <i>Then</i> you find out who the characters actually are, after you've established a social and genre context for them.</div> </div> </div> </div> I've heard this referred to as &quot;Session Zero&quot;, and that's pretty much what I do. That's where I gave a quick rundown of what sort of a game it would be (I like the idea of everyone deciding on that sort of thing together, but as long as I'm putting in 99% of the effort to make it happen, I'll be deciding what 99% of the game's nature will be).<br /> <br /> I've been doing this for over twenty years. I'm no stranger to the oddball character concept. And I can usually work with players to get somewhere we both feel comfortable.<br /> I guess the issue here is twofold. The first part, the most obvious, is that it is just so off-theme for what I'm trying to do. We want to tell a story about small town America, about isolation and wilderness and secrets. Not...all this other stuff.<br /> But the second part is where I think the real issue lies, and it took me ruminating over it on here to figure it out. This character concept isn't a concept at all. It's a character. Fully formed and grown. There's really no where for me to go with it.<br /> <br /> The player offered to shelve the character again...and then presented me with what they'd like to play as their second choice...which is just as over-developed and out of place as the first. No, you cannot begin the game as the head of a pyramid scheme selling baked goods. No, you cannot be a high-ranking member of the mafia. Sigh.<br /> <br /> I don't know. Am I wrong in that a regular ol' game of CoD is about...more or less regular people? Like, I played a guy who caught a glimpse of the world behind the curtain and was obsessed with it, but I kept the details vague and let other things fill in the gaps. And he was obsessed and paranoid because of the incident...but it was just one incident, not &quot;over 50&quot;. It's just a situation I've never come across before. Interesting and very frustrating.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26844-quixote","name":"Quixote","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-24T11:02:08-07:00","id":1449471,"title":"","content":"IME with this you've got two choices (or one and a half really):<br /> <br /> 1) Bite the bullet and pull out your No card. It sucks, because as a GM/ST/etc. you want to make the game fun for your players, and work their ideas into the game. But you have to look out for you, and the group, and if one person isn't willing to bend, you've got to do the right thing by everyone else. It's a shitty situation, but intellectually you know it's not your fault, and the good game you'll have in the end will make up for it.<br /> <br /> 2) Give the player one last shot at a PC: but they have to make the character with you, not present a full character for your approval. Something is not getting through between you in communication around expectations, so the only way to find out where is to go through the process together. This is a huge commitment of time and energy. It rarely actually works. Make it clear that option #1 there is the result of this process breaking down (giving out ultimatums sucks, but it's your only real leverage). Confrontation is bound to happen, so be prepared with things like, &quot;50 is way too many for me to make matter in the game, can you pick five that you really want to see come up later?&quot; and make sure to constantly challenge them to justify things not in terms of their character, but the premise of the game and as much as possible with meshing with the other PCs. Obviously, sometimes you get surprised and there's a good answer in there and you end up saying yes to stuff that you wouldn't have on its face before. But be far more ready to end up back at #1 anyway.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-24T13:32:24-07:00","id":1449485,"title":"","content":"Well, I figured: they asked for honesty, so I have it to them.<br /> <br /> I explained why I didn't feel like this character would fit. That characters in ttrpg are developed within the game, not fully formed outside of it. That a starting character should inspire scenes and stories, rather than have a ton of them already under their belt.<br /> And that I was concerned that the specific themes the character presented weren't really gelling with those that were rising up from the setting and other character concepts.<br /> <br /> So I invited the player to convert either of their characters to more broad, open-ended concepts or to come up with a new one.<br /> They took the second option. I'm...reasonably certain the player feels snubbed. But crisis averted, at least.<br /> <br /> Thanks for letting me spill all this on here and for affirming what I was feeling in my gut.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26844-quixote","name":"Quixote","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-02T06:35:14-07:00","id":1454073,"title":"","content":"My personal recommendation is to ask people to create a <b>list of ideas</b> in note form first. The next step ist talking about those ideas together and add changes where needed. If you can't find a solution for every &quot;problem&quot; right away, you might need another meeting. Finally, when everything works for both sides, the player sits down to write his character background.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Waiting for new Chronicles of Darkness Dice! \ud83e\udd17 \ud83c\udfb2","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1825-elythia","name":"Elythia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1825_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"100"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-02T10:44:41-07:00","id":1454088,"title":"","content":"I have had problems with this in the past and I now have a rule at my table: Your characters background cannot be longer than a paragraph (100 words or less if I have to get strict.). I want basic highlights and ideas only. We can then flesh out the details of the characters background during play and as a reaction to events in the game.<br /> <br /> I feel a highly elaborate and detailed background is antithesis of organic play and boxes in the player and can even disconnect them from the plot and game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12488-jeremysbrain","name":"Jeremysbrain","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"209"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-06T05:10:48-07:00","id":1454339,"title":"","content":"This sounds to me like a situation where they have had a character in mind for some time and are hoping your game is a chance to play them. They might take it personally if you say no, but they're also unwilling to make changes up front so I'd be worried that they might be upset if the game takes the character in a direction other than the great destiny they have in mind.<br /> <br /> I also have some concern about the child abuse angel. Maybe they're trying to work in something important front their own experience, or maybe they just want a traumatic backstory. But either way, you don't know what other players have experienced, so even if they play a different character, I'd set very clear standards for setting and communicating boundaries.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24383-laughinggull","name":"LaughingGull","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24383_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"30"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-07-07T19:58:14-07:00","id":1454497,"title":"","content":"You could always encourage them to continue developing said character backstory/mythology into its full novel form. (Not that this would help the game any, but might assuage any potential feelings of being snubbed.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-14T16:18:45-07:00","id":1456839,"title":"","content":"Overall you did the right thing. As hard as it is, the NO card sometimes must be used for the benefit of the group as a whole. Even if at some point it means a NO for the recurring participation of a player. Not all games are for everyone. Also your point on a beginning character being a conduit for stories and not a lot of stories already set is a good one, there are caveats, but it was, indeed, part of the problem.<br /> <br /> But there is one thing I want to add to the table:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Quixote</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1449412-character-concepts-and-storytellers#post1449412\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It seems to be an issue specifically with modern game settings; you have some players who only know how to make wizards and knights and other over-the-top characters with suitably over-the-top backgrounds. A character concept like &quot;jaded ex-cop&quot; or &quot;paranoid cryptozoologist&quot; seems boring to them. But I feel like modern horror games come to life when more or less regular folk are forced to face extraordinary circumstances.</div> </div> </div> </div>Here you have a lot of prejudice. Every single bit here is wrong.<br /> <br /> Let me make it clear that I have very similar tastes to you in this regard. The approach of &quot;common people, uncommon circumstances&quot; is my favorite one by far and I do find those over-the-top characters frequently annoying. But that is just our taste.<br /> <br /> It have nothing to do with genre or setting. Not even with realism, since Mafia bosses and over-the-top-sad-tale-Olympic-athletes and similar persons are actually a thing that happens. A one-in-a-million personal history happens 9 times in NYC alone. But common people facing extraordinary circumstances and over-the-top people facing their Hero's Journey both exist in every genre, setting and system. Mixing them also happen.<br /> <br /> The Lord of the Rings, the fundamental example of a Fantasy quest, out of nine members of the Fellowship 6 are as unremarkable as it gets, as the four Hobbits, the Dwarf and the Elf aren't hardened adventurers, just regular guys of their respective societies, that raised to the challenge when someone had to and their betters couldn't. In horror we have over-the-top badasses like Ellen Ripley in Alien, the unnamed man in They Live, Ash Williams in the Evil Dead series and Van Helsing since the original Dracula.<br /> <br /> You don't have to change your tastes. If your story doesn't bode well with over-developed characters that is perfectly fine. But you also may be limiting your view on things too much and missing themes and stories because of a narrow view fueled by prejudice, as I did in the past.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-15T16:53:19-07:00","id":1456912,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1449412-character-concepts-and-storytellers?p=1456839#post1456839\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Lord of the Rings, the fundamental example of a Fantasy quest, out of nine members of the Fellowship 6 are as unremarkable as it gets, as the four Hobbits, the Dwarf and the Elf aren't hardened adventurers, just regular guys of their respective societies, that raised to the challenge when someone had to and their betters couldn't.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you end up disproving your point more than making it here.<br /> <br /> Gimli is a noble. He can directly trace his bloodline to a king, though his ancestors were the second sons. All of Thorin II's companions (including Gimili's father) are members of the same extended noble family. It's not a king and some random vassals. It's a king and his noble cousins. Gimli might be distant from the throne, but he's certainly of the highest social standing.<br /> <br /> Legolas is the highest social ranked character in the Fellowship (at least at the start of it) as the crowned prince of the woodland elves. He's the sole son and heir of Thranduil. Aragorn and Gandalf surpass him in standing over the course of the story, but mostly because Legolas sails West before taking over as king (and presumably all of that plays into the elves leaving Middle Earth).<br /> <br /> Frodo, Merry, and Pippin are all rich landowners from prominent and politically powerful families (really the same larger extended family) in the Shire. Think about how wealthy they have to be by the standards of a pre-industrial agrarian society to just go off on an adventure without a worry at all about who's going to harvest the crops on their lands. Their job is owning land other people work on. If you were running a Shire focused game, asking to play on of them is like asking to play a Kennedy or some other heir of a prominent family with generations of power and money. Even Samwise, the most working class member of the Fellowship, has a job. Again, put this in its context. When most hobbits are farmers with no room for upwards mobility, Sam is a gardener for a wealthy family. He gets paid, so he can buy things he wants instead of only having what he grows/makes. The hobbits in the Fellowship feel like nobodies because the story doesn't really want to focus on how much they're absolutely not within their respective society and how they're surrounded by nobles not just wealthy people.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-15T22:23:30-07:00","id":1456932,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1449412-character-concepts-and-storytellers?p=1456912#post1456912\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think you end up disproving your point more than making it here.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, because the question wasn't &quot;poor characters&quot;, it was &quot;over-the-top backgrounds&quot;. Most of them led uneventful lives as it goes for their races and social standing. If you presented Legolas' background at the beginning of a table it would fit a small paragraph, and his concept is just &quot;Elf Prince&quot;. The dwarves, either Gimli or Thorin's company, were mostly the same thing, at most they had their genealogy tree drawn. It isn't like being the Mob Boss that conquered a city and more like being the Boss' heir that is yet to see a single crime in person.<br /> <br /> Thorin would be a counter-example. Before the Hobbit he already had under his belt the escape from Smaug (something most of the others hadn't), the war against the Orcs, the feats in the Battle of Azanulbizar that granted him the Oakenshield epithet and becoming king when his father wandered to Dol Guldur. Those are all extraordinary events, and that is the point.<br /> <br /> Legolas, Gimli and the Hobbits had no extraordinary stories under their belt before the start of LotR. Gandalf and Aragorn, on the other hand, where well established &quot;adventurers&quot; with strange journeys and incredible feats under their belts. They start the Fellowship already considered capable warriors prepared to face this journey and the forces of Sauron, at least to a degree, while the others are more like &quot;maybe they survive if they keep a low profile&quot;. Boromir was already a war hero at Gondor and made the journey to Rivendell by himself, a journey far harsher from there than from the Shire, and for him the journey is also just backstory.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-16T01:25:11-07:00","id":1456938,"title":"","content":"That's the problem though. They're still not &quot;regular guys&quot; that haven't done anything. They're just boring compared to the much bigger stuff Gandalf and Aragorn have done (I find the claim that Boromir had really done that much more than Legolas kinda off since Legolas is Thranduil's official messenger and thus regularly traveled alone across Middle Earth). The statement that, &quot;...just regular guys of their respective societies, that raised to the challenge when someone had to and their betters couldn't.&quot; doesn't hold up given who they actually are. Legolas and Gimli are never presented as untested in battle that will be lucky to survive, and both repeatedly engage enemies without any of the issues first time combatants would face.<br /> <br /> You say that Aragorn and Boromir have these amazing tales under their belts... but what are they? Aragorn did a bunch of stuff, but none of it important enough to get any details. What isn't summed up in, &quot;Secret heir of great power that led his small tribe in the harsh north after becoming an adult, and eventually took his rangers to watch the Shire for Gandalf.&quot; Boromir, &quot;son of the Stewart of Gondor, who survived a battle that killed everyone else in his unit save his brother as the highlight of the war he fought in.&quot;<br /> <br /> But the bigger issue is that &quot;setting&quot; here matters. If you're running a game about everyday hobbits of the Shire, Frodo's background is over-the-top: grew up in Buckland before being adopted nephew of the eccentric Bilbo Baggins (making him well traveled within the Shire), heir to the family's wealth and his uncle's collected treasures from traveling abroad (including a magical ring), friend of Gandalf the Grey, well studied and even knew Elvish, and had managed Bag End for 17 years (remember he was 50 when he left the Shire) on his own. Is that &quot;eventful&quot; for the Fellowship? Not really, but in the context of Shire society he's basically one of a kind.with more backstory elements and talents than most other exceptional hobbits combined.<br /> <br /> What's an over-the-top background for one setting or genre isn't inherently the same for a different setting or genre even if set in the same physical locations. Frodo is over-the-top for a slice of life story of the Shire, but trying-to-keep-up with the grander characters for the epic quest of the Fellowship from Rivendell to Mordor. Genre and setting clearly do matter to how a character feels in terms of appropriateness to a game. You're not really making the case against the idea that people used to Aragorn as &quot;normal PC material,&quot; might struggle to tone things back in a game where Samwise is &quot;normal PC material,&quot; Merry and Pippin are pushing it, and Frodo is over the top.<br /> <br /> If anything, you're making the opposite case where characters that simply didn't get a lot of attention in the main story are treated as &quot;unremarkable&quot; and &quot;regular joes&quot; despite how they'd look in a different context. Even if not part of the official tales, Legolas' expanded role in the Hobbit movies doesn't actually contradict the books or Tolkien's writing (which included notes about being annoyed that Legolas was frequently illustrated as being overly waif-ish instead of more rugged as his strength and experience should present him), and wouldn't be out of place at all if you wanted to tell Legolas's story in more detail (since the idea that he wouldn't have been at the Battle of Five Armies is kind of hard to defend). Gimli might not have been in any wars by the start of LotR, but he's still the son of a war hero (Gloin fought at Anazulbizar, and of course the Battle of the Five Armies), that wanted to join the quest to retake Erebor but was denied for being too young (kind of odd since at the time he was older than Gloin was when Gloin fought in the War of Dwarves and Orcs), and clearly continued his desire to prove himself in more then skirmishes when he joined the Fellowship. These are still characters with plenty of backstory, and enough backstory that means they may or may not fit the tone of a given game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-08-24T03:50:19-07:00","id":1457563,"title":"","content":"I think you misunderstand my core point. I may be wrong, by the way.<br /> <br /> For me it is pointless, to begin with, to qualify a character's backstory development outside the context of the story this character will be a part of. Let me try to re-contextualize directly from the OP:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Quixote</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1449412-character-concepts-and-storytellers#post1449412\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A character concept like &quot;jaded ex-cop&quot; or &quot;paranoid cryptozoologist&quot; seems boring to them.</div> </div> </div> </div>A jaded ex-cop by definition isn't someone new to combat and shit happening. This person already went through a lot. On most small-town stories this isn't absolutely less experienced than Legolas is for LotR. But it is still a two-word concept that don't need more than a paragraph to have a decent backstory (worth as introduction to the reader) or a single page if you want to make a detailed account if this is your style.<br /> <br /> If this character is instead developed to embrace its role as the champion of town even before the story begins, the author may decide on an extensive backstory with detailed accounts of feats, injuries, the event that made the character so jaded and hint to how he's destined to overcome his cynicism and become the hero the system never let him be. Then this character enters Aragorn's territory.<br /> <br /> Personally I don't see a paranoid cryptozoologist as much better, it's already a pretty niche area of study that hints to a person with fairly out-of-ordinary experience and knowledge, and the paranoia hints to a lot more having happened to this character before. It could be just as easily expanded into the hero that already had a brush with the Elder Gods and survived, maybe more than once. But those are the &quot;simple backgrounds&quot; used as examples.<br /> <br /> Legolas is a more experienced <i>person</i> than Aragorn will ever be. He is Thranduil's heir and messenger, traveled far and wide, fought in wars and generally lived in Mirkwood, with is pretty metal by itself, you should respect even the squirrels in that place. He is more than a thousand years old IIRC, or at least several centuries, while Aragorn is in his middle 70s. But for the <i>story</i> he is &quot;Elf Prince&quot;, you can make a decent BG to present him do the reader in a small paragraph or make a page if you want to be detailed, but you don't need this.<br /> <br /> While Aragorn is &quot;Heir of Legendary King, destined to be Returned King, Ranger Extraordinaire, Guardian of Excalibur-like, Protector of the North and Reliving Legend (because of how his relationship with Arwen is a reenacting of L\u00fathien and Beren's tale)&quot; from the get go, with poems and prophecies and whatnot about him, <b><i>and it is all relevant</i></b>. You simply can't shorten this for his role in LotR. The exact same person, if most of this weren't as important or thrown in the reader's face, could be easily a character concept like &quot;Ranger Extraordinaire&quot;, or &quot;Lost Prince&quot;, and be done with without changing anything about the <i>person</i> portrayed.<br /> <br /> On the other hand:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Quixote</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1449412-character-concepts-and-storytellers#post1449412\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They give me effective pages of character history that involves multiple multiples of child abuse, mental illness and near-death experiences.<br /> What's more, they give me the beginnings of a whole mythology; this Greater Destiny the character seems preordained for.</div> </div> </div> </div>You see, the problem isn't the character being an extraordinary person. PCs are extraordinary people, that's their normal, the starting sheet doesn't even let you really make a truly common person. But this isn't just an extraordinary person, this is what the OP described as &quot;over-the-top character with over-the-top background&quot;. And that is what the problem really is, the character have a lot of <i><b>story</b></i> baggage before the main story begins. It is overly developed and linked to an entire role in a larger mythos from the get go, like Aragorn, but definitely not like Legolas.<br /> <br /> By the way, it having battle experience wasn't even a thing in the OP complaint, it seems to still have far less of this than that jaded cop. But significantly more story weight.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":5826,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","user":"monteparnas","date":".  Added the ages of characters 08-24-2021, 06:53 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-03T19:32:38-07:00","id":1462603,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1449412-character-concepts-and-storytellers?p=1456839#post1456839\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Here you have a lot of prejudice. Every single bit here is wrong.</div> </div> </div> </div>I mean. I said it *seems*, that I *feel*--I wasn't trying to make any claims about the laws of ttrpg's or anything. Just articulating some theories based on my experience and how they affect my preferences.<br /> <br /> I am absolutely biased; I've been doing this for ages and I have sort of whittled down things to the style that works for me, that helps me set up the narratives that I find most enjoyable/impactful. And I am sure to tell potential players about such things at session 0, in case they don't want in on my particular brand.<br /> <br /> With that said, I understand what you're getting at.<br /> And to be clear, the two examples I gave were my attempt to think of a character in ChroD that has some instant appeal, some hook right out of the gate, versus something like &quot;teen grocery clerk&quot; or &quot;soccer mom&quot;.<br /> <br /> My issue with the player wasn't that his character was too complex or too much, really. It was that he'd written about so many things that sounded like story seeds in the character's background, and they were so specific. I want to include seeds like that, but when they're so well-defined as they are, there's not a lot of room for me to manuever within the story I have already started working on, you know?<br /> ...it...may also have something to do with the background coming across as...just tacky or poorly written, too. I suppose I can admit that much.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26844-quixote","name":"Quixote","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-03T21:40:35-07:00","id":1462615,"title":"","content":"That's understandable. I said that because I've seen your bias kind of get in the way of you noticing the real problem. A problem I face a lot with some players, too...<br /> <br /> What this guy delivered that time weren't characters. Were semi-complete stories that didn't left room for your story.<br /> <br /> I have a player that frequently wants to be THE BEST at something. And she means it. Isn't the best at the table, is THE BEST. If it isn't at least recognized as the best at a national level she feels frustrated for quite some time. Sometimes (when her anxiety is too out of control) it is at more than a couple things. And I have to navigate this, which is hard. Because it frequently is something the NPCs will have to be good at.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-04T11:25:56-07:00","id":1462678,"title":"","content":"Eh, maybe. I mean, the difference between &quot;bias&quot; and &quot;preference&quot; might be worth noting, here. I don't think there's anything wrong with knowing what you like and playing to your strengths.<br /> <br /> I think that I had multiple issues with the character concept, each one separate and valid (clash of tone, disregard of session 0, overall quality of the concept). It's almost like they increased my irritation and frustration exponentially.<br /> I've seen bad players. I've been in bad games. I'm done with all that. My free time is a precious and rare commodity, and I won't waste it.<br /> <br /> That sounds like an...interesting player. I don't know if I could roll at a table with someone like that. I'd give it a try, but. Yeah.<br /> I would be interested in figuring out why they felt that way. But that's less of a gamemastering thing and more of a therapy thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26844-quixote","name":"Quixote","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}